            "is_free": false,
            "detailed_description": "<h2 class=\"bb_tag\" >Eight friends. One screen. No survivors.</h2>Dog Fight is a couch party game about tiny planes and terrible decisions. Grab up to eight<br>controllers, pick a side, and turn the living room into a warzone. Rounds are short, the controls<br>take about four seconds to learn, and the loser always wants one more go.<h2 class=\"bb_tag\" >Play together, on one screen</h2><ul class=\"bb_ul\"><li><strong>Up to 8 players locally</strong> — versus, in teams, or co-operatively.<br></li><li><strong>Bots fill the empty seats</strong> so a two-player night still feels like a full lobby, and you<br>can practise against them solo.<br></li><li><strong>Pickups</strong> scattered through every arena swing a dogfight in a second — grab first, aim<br>later.</li></ul><h2 class=\"bb_tag\" >Four modes</h2><ul class=\"bb_ul\"><li><strong>Invasion</strong> — team up and hold off endless waves of ever-amassing enemies. Co-op, and the<br>one that runs longest.<br></li><li><strong>Team Free-for-All</strong> — pick sides and fight it out.<br></li><li><strong>Tag Team</strong> — one pilot up at a time, tag out when you're in trouble.<br></li><li><strong>Rabbit</strong> — one player is the rabbit, everyone else wants what they've got.</li></ul><h2 class=\"bb_tag\" >Online and LAN too</h2>Everything plays over the internet or a local network as well, so the couch can be a metaphor when<br>it has to be. But Dog Fight was built for the room, and that's where it's best.<h2 class=\"bb_tag\" >Also from bitshift</h2>If you like the sound of something slower, <strong>Trains &amp; Things</strong> is our real-time economic<br>strategy game — build a rail empire from a single track to a continent-spanning logistics network.<br>Both games are available together in the Bitshift Complete Pack.<br>",
            "about_the_game": "<h2 class=\"bb_tag\" >Eight friends. One screen. No survivors.</h2>Dog Fight is a couch party game about tiny planes and terrible decisions. Grab up to eight<br>controllers, pick a side, and turn the living room into a warzone. Rounds are short, the controls<br>take about four seconds to learn, and the loser always wants one more go.<h2 class=\"bb_tag\" >Play together, on one screen</h2><ul class=\"bb_ul\"><li><strong>Up to 8 players locally</strong> — versus, in teams, or co-operatively.<br></li><li><strong>Bots fill the empty seats</strong> so a two-player night still feels like a full lobby, and you<br>can practise against them solo.<br></li><li><strong>Pickups</strong> scattered through every arena swing a dogfight in a second — grab first, aim<br>later.</li></ul><h2 class=\"bb_tag\" >Four modes</h2><ul class=\"bb_ul\"><li><strong>Invasion</strong> — team up and hold off endless waves of ever-amassing enemies. Co-op, and the<br>one that runs longest.<br></li><li><strong>Team Free-for-All</strong> — pick sides and fight it out.<br></li><li><strong>Tag Team</strong> — one pilot up at a time, tag out when you're in trouble.<br></li><li><strong>Rabbit</strong> — one player is the rabbit, everyone else wants what they've got.</li></ul><h2 class=\"bb_tag\" >Online and LAN too</h2>Everything plays over the internet or a local network as well, so the couch can be a metaphor when<br>it has to be. But Dog Fight was built for the room, and that's where it's best.<h2 class=\"bb_tag\" >Also from bitshift</h2>If you like the sound of something slower, <strong>Trains &amp; Things</strong> is our real-time economic<br>strategy game — build a rail empire from a single track to a continent-spanning logistics network.<br>Both games are available together in the Bitshift Complete Pack.<br>",
            "short_description": "The ultimate couch party game. Grab up to 8 friends for frantic local air combat — duel head to head, team up, or hold off endless waves together. Four modes, bots to fill the empty seats. Online and LAN too.",
